{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And on the day raw flesh is seen in it.</b> If raw flesh grew in it — has He not already stated that raw flesh is a sign of impurity? Rather: it speaks of a plague-affection that was on one of the twenty-four tips of limbs that are not susceptible to impurity by reason of raw flesh — because the plague-affection is not seen all at once, since they slope this way and that (Negaim 6; Kiddushin 25) — and the tip of the limb turned back and its slope was exposed by means of a bandage, for instance if it healed and became broad and the raw flesh became visible in it — Scripture teaches that it is impure (Sifra)." "<b>And on the day.</b> What does Scripture tell us? To teach: there is a day on which you inspect it, and there is a day on which you do not inspect it. From here they said: For a bridegroom they grant all seven days of the feast — for him, for his garment, and for his house; and likewise on a festival they grant him all the days of the festival (Negaim 3; Moed Katan 7)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "ראשי אברים rendered 'tips of limbs'; the slope (שפוע) refers to the way the plague spreads unevenly across the limb-tip."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:06:10.357747455Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}